\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Pauli arrays of the flip and the sign}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{At #{i\varepsilon_1}, where #{\varepsilon_j} is the standard basis
of #{\mathbb C^d}, the spatial form has value #{-1}, while the
Hermitian norm squared is #{1}. In the ordered basis #{(e_0,e_1)}
of one factor of the
[centered Dirac operator](fdip-000R), and in the
[Pauli convention](fcap-002L), the flip and the sign have arrays
##{X=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
Z=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}.}
With #{I=I_2}, the product-index array of #{\gamma_j} is
##{\gamma_j=Z^{\otimes(j-1)}\otimes X
\otimes I^{\otimes(d-j)}.}
An empty tensor product contributes the scalar identity. The
identities #{X^{\dagger}=X}, #{Z^{\dagger}=Z}, #{X^2=Z^2=I}, #{XZ=-ZX} and the
Clifford relation are proved in \ref{fdip-00L8}. Mathlib's
\leanref{Matrix.kronecker},
\leanref{Matrix.mul_kronecker_mul},
\leanref{Matrix.conjTranspose_kronecker} and
\leanref{Matrix.one_kronecker_one} give the corresponding matrix
construction. Iterating them requires identifying the product-index
bases.}
